00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.680
TERRY CHO: Hello, everyone.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:11.690
OK, this is the
last session, right?

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:13.760
After the session, you
may go back to home

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.372
or I may have some
drinks with my friends.

00:00:18.372 --> 00:00:19.080
Nice to meet you.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.410
My name is Terry, Customer
Engineer from the Cloud team.

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:26.000
It is the honor to introduce
our Stackdriver logging

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.890
use case, Samsung.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:31.360
Before I introduce
our customer, I

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:36.200
will briefly introduce logging
itself and Stackdriver.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:37.940
OK, let's start.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:39.740
So what is the logging?

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.110
Logging is gathering
event and information

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:46.430
to see the state of the system.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.790
Up here, we just used the
logging for the troubleshooting

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:51.090
and the debugging.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:54.080
And we collect the
logs into a single file

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.240
and monitor by using
the tailing command.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.370
But logging can be used
for the multiple purpose.

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:04.379
For example, for
developer, logging

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:08.620
can be used for the debugging
and code optimization.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.620
For the system
engineer, it can be

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.850
used for the health check
and the system monitoring.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.680
For the security
engineer, it can

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:22.410
be used to protect the system
to find abnormal behavior.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:26.050
And nowadays, data, in general,
is being more important.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:32.700
So the data can be
gathered through the log

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.810
to find the insight.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:37.790
In this reason, we need
to collect more logs

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.050
from the various layers.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:43.370
For example, we can collect
the CPU and the memory status

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:45.980
from the infrastructure
and the OS level.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:49.790
And we can get a web
access log from the web

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:52.040
server or the database.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:55.490
Also, we can collect the log
from the usual application.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:02:00.110
These logs can be used for the
multiple purpose, like business

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:04.910
analytics, auditing,
billing, something like that.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:09.139
Because of that, we need
more [INAUDIBLE] terminal.

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:10.820
Until now, it may be OK.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:13.580
But the problem
is, system is being

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.530
moved to the distributed
architecture,

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:17.250
like microservices.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.930
Because of that, it may
have a lot of log files.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:24.470
So with a single monitor, we
cannot monitor the whole log.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:26.260
So what is the solution?

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:28.760
The solution is, we can use
more and more and more different

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.410
monitors, right?

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:33.440
But actually, it is
a technical solution.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.170
So I will introduce
a technical solution

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:40.770
to monitor the
distributed architecture.

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:44.820
Here is the general architecture
of the logging system.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.860
We can collect a
log from the system

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.310
and send it into the
message queue, like Kafka.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:54.380
And behind the message queue,
we can collect the log.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:58.330
The collected log will store
into the log storage system

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:00.060
for the archiving purpose.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.430
And the log will
be indexed by using

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:04.890
the indexer for such a purpose.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:08.790
So user can search
and view the log.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:10.980
But the problem
is, as I mentioned,

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:14.400
the log can be used for the
multiple purpose, like data

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.580
analytics or system
notification.

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.360
And nowadays, machine learning
is more being important.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.620
So the log data
can be [INAUDIBLE]

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:28.130
by the machine learning
system to train the model.

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:34.480
This system can be
implemented by yourself,

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.290
where you can implement
this system by compositing

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:40.310
multiple open source.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:44.020
But the problem is, it takes
long time to log, build,

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:47.750
and you can also bring
a operation of overload.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:50.160
So what is the solution?

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:51.750
Google has Stackdriver.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:55.740
Stackdriver is operation
and monitoring system.

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:59.820
It combines the logging,
monitoring, debugging,

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:01.590
trace, et cetera.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.860
If you didn't have a chance
to investigate Stackdriver,

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.800
I strongly recommend you
to visit the demo booth.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.870
I saw that there are a
lot of Stackdriver demo.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:14.250
It is really awesome tool.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:17.910
In addition, the Stackdriver
can run over the multiple cloud

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:21.210
infrastructure, including the
Google Cloud and the Amazon

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:23.200
service.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.570
Today, I will just focus
on the logging one.

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:29.560
In the Stackdriver logging,
it can support the logging

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:30.880
collection post.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:34.840
It can collect the log by
using the SDK or some agent.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:36.860
The collected log
will be stored,

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:39.350
and it can be
searched and viewed

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:43.670
in the built-in Stackdriver
Logging console.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:46.130
And the collective
log can be routed

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:48.220
to the multiple destination.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:52.580
Stackdriver can be
worked log router.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:57.080
For example, it is very similar
to the Kafka in open source.

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:01.290
From the log file, we
can extract the metric.

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:03.840
For example, we can count
the number of the error,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:09.412
or we can extract the overall
response time of our service.

00:05:09.412 --> 00:05:15.500
We define this metric, we can
also define the notification.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:18.140
For example, if we have
some number of incident,

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:21.560
you can send us a notification
message to the system operator

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:27.020
by using the email, or the
SMS, or a page to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:29.780
And the last thing
is, log management

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:32.480
for the multiple project.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:35.120
Now this, as I mentioned,
the system architecture

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:37.410
is being changed to
the distributed way.

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.260
So the application
can be deployed

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:42.890
into the multiple cloud
project, in case of Google,

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:46.670
and multiple account,
in case of Amazon.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:50.170
If we have to monitor the all
log row in different project

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:53.880
and in different cloud concept,
it may be very, very painful.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:56.270
So the Stackdriver can
collect all of logs

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:59.750
into the single place, and
you can monitor all logs

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:03.020
in a single plane of glass.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:06.880
OK, let's deep dive each
log, into a picture.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:09.710
The first thing is how
we collect the log.

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:12.460
The Stackdriver
provides the SDK.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:17.470
The SDK can be easily integrated
with a well-known logging

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:17.990
framework.

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:22.670
For example, in case of Java,
[INAUDIBLE] and the logback

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:24.490
is usually used.

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:26.750
This is the Java example.

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:32.730
As you can see, there
are no code changes.

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:35.850
You just simply change
the Maven dependency

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:38.970
to inject the Stackdriver
Logging library.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:41.220
It is really easy.

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:43.710
But unfortunately,
sometimes, we need

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:45.960
to collect the log from
the built-in solution,

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:48.180
like a database or a web server.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:51.990
Or sometimes, your logging
framework cannot be supported

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:53.220
by the Stackdriver.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:57.720
In that case, we can use
the Stackdriver agent,

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:00.670
which is based on the
fluentd open source agent.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:05.700
So you can collect the log from
the file by using this agent.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:09.270
After you collect the log,
you can easily search and view

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:12.710
the log by using the Stackdriver
logging built-in console.

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:14.500
It's really easy, and
it can also support

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:15.750
coding language like this one.

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:21.260
After log has been
collected, as I mentioned,

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:23.680
for the multiple
purpose uses, it

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:27.440
can be routed to the
multiple destinations.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:32.030
Like Google Cloud Storage, it is
for the log archiving purpose.

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:35.060
And the Pub/Sub can be
used for the log streaming.

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:37.910
So behind the Pub/Sub,
you can put the real time

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:41.120
in our [INAUDIBLE] system or
in some transformation system

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.860
for the data permitting.

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:46.730
Or you can export the log
into the BigQuery for the data

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:48.080
analytics.

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:51.140
You can export the log to
the multiple destinations

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:52.610
based on the filtering.

00:07:52.610 --> 00:07:56.240
So it means that you can
selectively export the log

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:58.730
to the multiple destinations.

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:01.940
In addition, basically,
Stackdriver logging pricing

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:05.640
is based on the stored log size.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:07.740
If you have to
store a lot of logs,

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:11.190
it can bring a cost issue.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:14.360
So the Stackdriver also
provides exclusion filter,

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:17.480
which means that you can
selectively store the log based

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:18.950
on the filter.

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:20.525
And you can save the cost.

00:08:24.370 --> 00:08:27.170
Nowadays, the logging
has more meaningful.

00:08:27.170 --> 00:08:30.170
I mean, a few years
ago, we just logging

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:31.230
for the troubleshooting.

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:33.340
So the text message was enough.

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:36.320
But the developer
tried to condition

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:40.640
some data, like the user ID,
and the transaction ID, price

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:41.789
from the log.

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:44.150
In that case, the
structured logging format

00:08:44.150 --> 00:08:50.230
is more effective, like JSON
format or the CSV format.

00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:53.240
Stackdriver supports
the JSON formatting.

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:55.540
This is the node.js example.

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:58.700
If you write the log
with JSON format,

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:02.420
it is automatically met to
the JSON payload element

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:05.760
in the Stackdriver log entry.

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:10.800
And if you export this
JSON log into the BigQuery,

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:13.560
each element in the
JSON payload will

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:16.760
be mapped to the column
in the BigQuery table.

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:20.010
So it helps you to more
searchable and readable.

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:27.620
From the stored log, we
can define the metric.

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:30.080
We already have some
predefined metrics, which

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:32.570
is called as a system metric.

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:35.315
We can get a data,
like a byte count

00:09:35.315 --> 00:09:38.130
or the number of log entries.

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:40.550
But if you wanted to
collect specific information

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:44.850
from your log, you can define
custom log in two ways.

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:47.140
The first way is
the counter metrics.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:49.550
It is a simple counter
metric, but you

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:51.890
can define some conditions.

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:55.400
For example, I want to
collect the number of logs

00:09:55.400 --> 00:10:00.810
that web latency is
over than 1.0 seconds.

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:04.400
And the other way is
distribution metric.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:06.640
It records the
statistical distribution,

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:10.880
like standard deviation,
sum, averages.

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:15.700
For example, it can collect
the average response

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:18.660
time of your service.

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:22.920
And then this is an example
of how you define the metric.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:24.400
It is really easy.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:29.370
And with this metric, you
can visualize the metric

00:10:29.370 --> 00:10:33.510
by using the graph through the
Stackdriver monitoring console.

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:36.350
There is one example of
the web response time,

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:38.260
and this is a heatmap graph.

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:41.897
You can get some insight
about your system status

00:10:41.897 --> 00:10:42.480
like this one.

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:50.610
After we define the metric, we
can make some alerting system.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:53.540
With predefined
rules, the Stackdriver

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:57.220
will send some notification
to the various destinations

00:10:57.220 --> 00:10:59.260
from the email, SMS.

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:02.347
And you can send a notification
through your application

00:11:02.347 --> 00:11:03.430
through the Pub/Sub queue.

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:08.290
Last one is centralized logging.

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:12.110
As I mentioned, in
microservice architecture,

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:15.990
each service can be deployed
to the different systems,

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:18.100
and different
products in Google,

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:21.310
and the different
Amazon accounts.

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:25.510
So collecting the log into
some centralized system

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:27.350
is very, very important.

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:31.100
So Stackdriver can
collect all of logs

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:34.220
into the one GCP
product and can provide

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:38.110
single plane of the glass
for the log monitoring.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:42.680
OK, until now, we quickly looked
through the logging system

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:44.210
with Stackdriver.

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:49.340
But logging system can be
extended for various purpose,

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:51.220
like big data analytics.

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:55.900
It is a way how we can
extend the logging system.

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:59.240
In the real world, when
we try to use the log

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:05.200
data for the big data analytics,
actual data is really dirty.

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:08.110
I mean that it doesn't
follow the format,

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:11.230
and it doesn't follow
the type sometimes.

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:13.870
And sometimes,
the field is null.

00:12:13.870 --> 00:12:17.470
So we need the data validation
and the transformation.

00:12:17.470 --> 00:12:21.010
In Stackdriver, we
can export the logging

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:23.970
into the Pub/Sub behind
the Stackdriver logging

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:26.650
and [INAUDIBLE] by the Dataflow.

00:12:26.650 --> 00:12:29.720
Dataflow is open source
version of the Apache,

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:34.180
which is very similar to
the Apache Spark Streaming.

00:12:34.180 --> 00:12:36.820
And it can be used for
the big data analytics.

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:40.420
So you can put some
validation and transformation

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:42.700
logic in the Google Dataflow.

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:46.750
But unfortunately, Dataflow
requires some coding

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:49.390
by using the Java and Python.

00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:52.660
It requires little bit
tiny learning curve.

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:55.540
However, instead of
your manual coding,

00:12:55.540 --> 00:12:58.690
Stackdriver provides
a template feature.

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:00.940
It means that you
don't need to code.

00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:06.560
By a number of clicking in
the Google Cloud console,

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:09.340
you can make a data
injection pipeline.

00:13:09.340 --> 00:13:14.640
In addition, if you need to
add some transformation logic,

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:17.220
you can simply
implement it by using

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:22.980
JavaScript, which is named the
UDF, User Defined Function.

00:13:22.980 --> 00:13:26.670
So the user defined function
can be uploaded to the GCS

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:30.360
without any packaging, like
[INAUDIBLE] in the Java.

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:34.710
So you just simply unload
it and point it out

00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:36.840
from the Dataflow console.

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:41.010
During the Dataflow run, it will
pull the transformation logic

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:42.520
and convert the data format.

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:48.790
By using the
Stackdriver logging,

00:13:48.790 --> 00:13:51.460
we can collect the log
from the server side.

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:54.160
But in big data
analytics world, we

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:56.980
wanted to collect
all of user journey,

00:13:56.980 --> 00:14:00.920
from the advertisement,
marketing, mobile, like that.

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:04.450
Fortunately, Google
provided us a solution.

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:07.320
For mobile, Firebase
[INAUDIBLE] can

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:10.090
gather all of user
event information

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:12.340
and send it into the BigQuery.

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:17.140
And for web, Google analytics
can collect all of user events

00:14:17.140 --> 00:14:18.220
from the site.

00:14:18.220 --> 00:14:22.400
In addition, BigQuery itself
has a lot of data connector.

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:28.060
We can collect advertisement,
network data, Google Play,

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:32.230
and YouTube data.

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:35.710
As I know, in this week,
Google announced a BigQuery

00:14:35.710 --> 00:14:38.330
additional connector
through the partner.

00:14:38.330 --> 00:14:42.310
So now, we can collect
over than 100 data

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:45.540
stores across SNS,
advertisement,

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:47.460
and the [INAUDIBLE] system.

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:50.500
So all of data can be
stored in BigQuery,

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:54.260
and you can analyze the user
behavior from end to end.

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:56.140
It's really cool, isn't it?

00:14:56.140 --> 00:14:56.867
No?

00:14:56.867 --> 00:15:00.140
Thank you.

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:02.530
OK, the other problem in the
microservice architecture

00:15:02.530 --> 00:15:06.190
is called relation
of the log record.

00:15:06.190 --> 00:15:09.600
The problem is, let's say that
the user A sends a request

00:15:09.600 --> 00:15:10.600
to the user service.

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:14.380
And it writes the log
into logger file system.

00:15:14.380 --> 00:15:17.260
And it will send the request
to the other service,

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:20.030
like an item service.

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:22.670
User B also calls same thing.

00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:25.740
But the problem is, I
want to track the user A's

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:27.020
request only.

00:15:27.020 --> 00:15:31.400
So how can correlate the user
service and the item service

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:32.610
log?

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:39.840
The way is, we can put some code
relation ID, which is trace ID.

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:42.930
So when user calls
the user service,

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:46.350
it will automatically
generate the trace ID

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:49.660
and store it in
the log together.

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:53.480
And the user service sends the
request to the item service,

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:56.880
this trace ID will be
propagated together

00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:00.750
and written to the log file.

00:16:00.750 --> 00:16:05.940
So by using this trace ID, you
can correlate the log entry

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:08.460
between the different services.

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:10.170
You can implement
it by yourself.

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:13.470
But fortunately, there are
open source named Zipkin.

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:16.530
If you are integrating Zipkin
open source into your logging

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:19.710
system, it will automatically
create a trace ID

00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:22.920
and propagate the ID
to the other system.

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:25.890
And the trace ID also
will automatically

00:16:25.890 --> 00:16:28.200
return to your
log file together.

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:33.110
Actually, the Zipkin is
not a logging system.

00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:38.030
It is originally designed to
support the distributed system

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:39.230
trace.

00:16:39.230 --> 00:16:42.770
It means that it can trace
the latency in distributed

00:16:42.770 --> 00:16:44.630
system by interval.

00:16:44.630 --> 00:16:48.770
For example, I call the user
service and item service.

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:52.130
But I want to know,
where is the bottleneck?

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:55.540
How long time does it
takes for the item service?

00:16:55.540 --> 00:16:58.400
After your Zipkin
integrations, Stackdriver also

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:02.700
support the Zipkin by
using Stackdriver trace.

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:06.849
Stackdriver trace will collect
this latency information

00:17:06.849 --> 00:17:11.950
and visualize by using
graphs like this one.

00:17:11.950 --> 00:17:12.980
This is really good.

00:17:12.980 --> 00:17:16.390
But the problem is,
to use the Zipkin,

00:17:16.390 --> 00:17:19.089
you need to inject
your Zipkin source

00:17:19.089 --> 00:17:21.220
code to your application.

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:25.099
But I wanted to collect
the other system latency,

00:17:25.099 --> 00:17:28.930
which cannot be injected by the
Zipkin, for example, database,

00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:30.220
web server.

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:33.600
But I wanted to check the trace.

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:36.970
In that case, we can
resolve this problem

00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:39.040
in the infrastructure area.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:43.670
Instead of fixing the
code, we can put some proxy

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:46.670
in front of the service,
which is Envoy proxy.

00:17:46.670 --> 00:17:49.610
It is very similar to
the [INAUDIBLE] proxy.

00:17:49.610 --> 00:17:51.800
But in the
microservice world, we

00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:56.600
need to deploy a lot of proxy
in front of each of service.

00:17:56.600 --> 00:18:01.610
Instead of that, istio
made Envoy service.

00:18:01.610 --> 00:18:08.360
It can automatically index the
Envoy in front of your service.

00:18:08.360 --> 00:18:12.430
So regarding the release of
the application programming

00:18:12.430 --> 00:18:14.690
language, you can collect
all of trace logs.

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:18.890
And these trace logs can be
integrated with Stackdriver

00:18:18.890 --> 00:18:21.050
service monitoring.

00:18:21.050 --> 00:18:23.170
As you can see, in the
microservice sector,

00:18:23.170 --> 00:18:25.990
the topology and the
service dependencies

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:27.860
are really, really complicated.

00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:30.410
But it shows the
topology like this one,

00:18:30.410 --> 00:18:34.070
and it also provides the
important information,

00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:38.020
like response time, error
rate, GPU utilization.

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:44.550
OK, final thing is
personal information.

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:48.300
Personal information is
very, very sensitive, right?

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:52.590
If you store the personal
information to the storage,

00:18:52.590 --> 00:18:57.330
if it is originally designed
for the personal information

00:18:57.330 --> 00:18:59.580
handling, you may
not have a problem.

00:18:59.580 --> 00:19:04.320
But sometimes, unexpectedly,
the PII or user information

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:06.780
can be stored in
your logging system.

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:08.460
It is critical.

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:11.460
To prevent that, we
can use the DLP API--

00:19:11.460 --> 00:19:14.395
Data Loss Prevention-- which
is based on the Google machine

00:19:14.395 --> 00:19:15.740
learning technology.

00:19:15.740 --> 00:19:19.320
It can automatically detect
the user information,

00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:21.360
like social security
number, what

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:22.770
are some other information.

00:19:22.770 --> 00:19:29.620
And it can anonymize,
make it as a hash value.

00:19:29.620 --> 00:19:33.570
So it can prevent
personal information

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:36.150
to be stored in
the logging system.

00:19:36.150 --> 00:19:41.240
OK, from last year, by using
the Stackdriver logging,

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:46.290
Samsung started to
move to the innovation

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:51.010
to SRE and the DevOps practice.

