the logging system is
usually for the debugging,

00:29:55.310 --> 00:30:01.130
and the monitoring, and also
the analysis purpose, too.

00:30:01.130 --> 00:30:06.350
But with the small size of
the project or the services

00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:10.550
can consider that, maybe just
to use the ELK for the logging

00:30:10.550 --> 00:30:14.690
system, or some kind of
just open source solutions

00:30:14.690 --> 00:30:16.330
to overcome the situation.

00:30:16.330 --> 00:30:23.120
However, some services that is
for customer service or larger

00:30:23.120 --> 00:30:28.370
scale services always try to
build their own logging system.

00:30:28.370 --> 00:30:31.410
That is for reliability
and scalability, too.

00:30:31.410 --> 00:30:37.400
And currently, the new
Bixby, the log data

00:30:37.400 --> 00:30:40.390
is almost 1.5 terabyte per day.

00:30:40.390 --> 00:30:43.100
And we have to
think about how we

00:30:43.100 --> 00:30:48.230
can satisfy the real-time
monitoring with using

00:30:48.230 --> 00:30:50.750
our current internal
logging system.

00:30:50.750 --> 00:30:56.990
And also, we have to
guarantee that the reliability

00:30:56.990 --> 00:31:00.950
and scalability, too, for
the customer services.

00:31:00.950 --> 00:31:05.540
So when we think about
the previous logging

00:31:05.540 --> 00:31:08.630
system in Bixby services,
the major key point

00:31:08.630 --> 00:31:14.150
was that logging for
debugging, and the other one

00:31:14.150 --> 00:31:17.550
is the cost of saving point.

00:31:17.550 --> 00:31:21.560
Actually, the previous
internal logging system

00:31:21.560 --> 00:31:25.340
was developed by ourselves.

00:31:25.340 --> 00:31:32.060
And that means that that is not
known solutions in the market.

00:31:32.060 --> 00:31:37.640
It was designed in our side and
developed in our development

00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:38.140
team.

00:31:38.140 --> 00:31:43.640
And that is deployed to
the cloud infrastructure.

00:31:43.640 --> 00:31:50.930
And mostly, all requirement was
driven by our internal users.

00:31:50.930 --> 00:31:55.250
So it is more easy to
satisfy all the requirements

00:31:55.250 --> 00:31:58.820
when we develop ourselves
for the logging system.

00:31:58.820 --> 00:32:03.500
However, when we want
to add new features,

00:32:03.500 --> 00:32:07.040
like the tracing or
E3 testing, then we

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:10.070
have to change our
infrastructure and the design

00:32:10.070 --> 00:32:11.990
concept of the logging system.

00:32:11.990 --> 00:32:15.410
But that system was not
designed at that time.

00:32:15.410 --> 00:32:20.300
And simply, they logged all log
messages into a logging system,

00:32:20.300 --> 00:32:24.830
and simple monitoring was
only supported by the Grafana.

00:32:24.830 --> 00:32:27.350
So we have some challenges.

00:32:27.350 --> 00:32:33.440
How we can provide the tracing
mechanisms to the developers

00:32:33.440 --> 00:32:39.830
to give some help how they
can debug the issues when

00:32:39.830 --> 00:32:43.170
some issues are detected
in their own applications?

00:32:43.170 --> 00:32:46.940
And indeed, when we are
adding the new logging system

00:32:46.940 --> 00:32:55.190
into our cloud infrastructure,
the complexity of the system

00:32:55.190 --> 00:32:58.460
goes much higher
than we expected,

00:32:58.460 --> 00:33:01.730
and it is not easy to
add in new features

00:33:01.730 --> 00:33:03.620
to the new logging system.

00:33:03.620 --> 00:33:06.800
As well as the cost
also goes up high.

00:33:06.800 --> 00:33:12.320
And the cost was one of
the big challenges for us

00:33:12.320 --> 00:33:15.380
to reduce the costing fee.

00:33:15.380 --> 00:33:19.220
So that is the
major tool request

00:33:19.220 --> 00:33:20.540
for the DevOps team side.

00:33:20.540 --> 00:33:23.150
And we have to
change, or we have

00:33:23.150 --> 00:33:27.680
to find out any solutions
available in the market.

00:33:27.680 --> 00:33:34.285
So we gathered some suggestions
from the other team.

00:33:34.285 --> 00:33:36.410
That wasn't at the [INAUDIBLE]
of Google, actually.

00:33:36.410 --> 00:33:40.690
We searched the Stackdriver
in the Google at the website,

00:33:40.690 --> 00:33:43.130
and we found that
we have to check

00:33:43.130 --> 00:33:47.360
that what is our key feature
for providing the new logging

00:33:47.360 --> 00:33:48.320
system.

00:33:48.320 --> 00:33:51.750
So firstly, we have to think
about the real-time monitoring.

00:33:51.750 --> 00:33:54.890
Stackdriver supports that
real-time monitoring is

00:33:54.890 --> 00:33:56.795
based on the log-based metrics.

00:34:01.690 --> 00:34:04.610
The logging system is
using the Stackdriver.

00:34:04.610 --> 00:34:08.600
It also can support the
real-time monitoring.

00:34:08.600 --> 00:34:12.650
But it has also
provided easy ways

00:34:12.650 --> 00:34:17.070
to integrate with the
other monitoring solutions.

00:34:17.070 --> 00:34:23.239
In the Bixby 1.0, we have the
internal monitoring solution,

00:34:23.239 --> 00:34:27.440
and that is also have
to be continuously

00:34:27.440 --> 00:34:30.060
to use for our operations.

00:34:30.060 --> 00:34:34.190
So we have to check whether
the Stackdriver can support

00:34:34.190 --> 00:34:37.790
the third-party monitoring
solution integration,

00:34:37.790 --> 00:34:42.469
and we can guarantee
that that is possible.

00:34:42.469 --> 00:34:48.139
And second, the most important
thing on the logging system

00:34:48.139 --> 00:34:50.570
is the experience of debugging.

00:34:50.570 --> 00:34:53.270
So now, our previous
logging system,

00:34:53.270 --> 00:34:56.929
when developers want
to search any logs,

00:34:56.929 --> 00:35:05.450
only the application-driven logs
can be searched at the time.

00:35:05.450 --> 00:35:10.250
The one problem was, all
the developers cannot make

00:35:10.250 --> 00:35:13.010
the standard log
format at a time.

00:35:13.010 --> 00:35:17.000
And a huge number of
developers in Samsung

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:19.520
was involved in this
Bixby development,

00:35:19.520 --> 00:35:22.600
and they made it
their own style.

00:35:22.600 --> 00:35:24.710
We made log messages.

00:35:24.710 --> 00:35:28.220
It doesn't show any
related log messages

00:35:28.220 --> 00:35:29.990
for debugging purposes.

00:35:29.990 --> 00:35:36.350
So it means that developers want
to check whether a new detected

00:35:36.350 --> 00:35:42.230
issue belongs to their problem
or to another other module's

00:35:42.230 --> 00:35:43.370
problem.

00:35:43.370 --> 00:35:48.590
We have to provide any tracing
mechanisms to developers

00:35:48.590 --> 00:35:52.940
for providing the easy
ways to search the log

00:35:52.940 --> 00:35:54.860
messages for debugging.

00:35:54.860 --> 00:35:57.920
So when we checked
that the Stackdriver,

00:35:57.920 --> 00:36:02.690
it also can support
the tracing mechanisms

00:36:02.690 --> 00:36:04.790
as using the automated trace.

00:36:04.790 --> 00:36:09.380
However, it cannot be applied
to our internal requirement.

00:36:09.380 --> 00:36:15.290
Actually, if we want to add
the auto-created tracing ID,

00:36:15.290 --> 00:36:17.390
we have to persuade
all the developers

00:36:17.390 --> 00:36:20.540
to apply that requirement
to their source code.

00:36:20.540 --> 00:36:21.770
It is not easy.

00:36:21.770 --> 00:36:25.880
I think some of you are
working in the big company.

00:36:25.880 --> 00:36:29.070
The big company have
several organizations.

00:36:29.070 --> 00:36:31.430
And even though that
organization only

00:36:31.430 --> 00:36:33.740
belongs to the development,
the development team

00:36:33.740 --> 00:36:36.650
have their own
strategy and policies.

00:36:36.650 --> 00:36:38.750
It is not easy to
add in the new code

00:36:38.750 --> 00:36:43.670
for only the tracing
purpose into their code.

00:36:43.670 --> 00:36:48.260
Because they worry that any
side effect and any performance

00:36:48.260 --> 00:36:53.120
degradation due to
adding the new mechanisms

00:36:53.120 --> 00:36:54.380
to their own code.

00:36:54.380 --> 00:36:58.070
So we have to make sure
that tracing is also

00:36:58.070 --> 00:37:01.130
need to be adding a
new logging system.

00:37:01.130 --> 00:37:03.710
And we made a new
standard log format

00:37:03.710 --> 00:37:07.190
to apply the Stackdriver.

00:37:07.190 --> 00:37:09.740
And secondly, previous
logging system

00:37:09.740 --> 00:37:14.090
also provides that the
analytics of the system.

00:37:14.090 --> 00:37:17.420
It means that some of the log
messages should be gathered,

00:37:17.420 --> 00:37:22.640
and that is sent to
another analytics platform.

00:37:22.640 --> 00:37:24.740
And that platform is
gathering all data

00:37:24.740 --> 00:37:27.350
and providing the analysis data.

00:37:27.350 --> 00:37:31.040
We have to make sure
that the Stackdriver have

00:37:31.040 --> 00:37:33.920
to support that,
and we had to check

00:37:33.920 --> 00:37:36.260
whether that is available.

00:37:36.260 --> 00:37:39.600
So after checking that
all the requirement,

00:37:39.600 --> 00:37:43.190
we can go with the Stackdriver
without any building

00:37:43.190 --> 00:37:45.590
the internal logging system.

00:37:45.590 --> 00:37:48.890
Internal logging system usually
requires too much effort

00:37:48.890 --> 00:37:49.910
to develop.

00:37:49.910 --> 00:37:54.890
And also, we cannot satisfy
that all the project milestones

00:37:54.890 --> 00:37:57.180
to go forward.

00:37:57.180 --> 00:37:59.140
So now, we could do that.

00:37:59.140 --> 00:38:02.780
We have to do that about the
log format standardization

00:38:02.780 --> 00:38:07.010
to satisfy that tracing purpose.

00:38:07.010 --> 00:38:10.310
The other one is that, usually,
when we build a new logging

00:38:10.310 --> 00:38:13.800
system, always, we have to
think about the user management.

00:38:13.800 --> 00:38:18.890
So when we use the Stackdriver,
all the user authentications

00:38:18.890 --> 00:38:24.680
or user management is
[INAUDIBLE] by the IIM policy.

00:38:24.680 --> 00:38:28.640
And the Stackdriver
log viewer is basically

00:38:28.640 --> 00:38:35.030
supported so that every user who
gets the permissions to access

00:38:35.030 --> 00:38:39.230
the log viewer pages,
then they can immediately

00:38:39.230 --> 00:38:45.500
search the log messages
by accessing the console.

00:38:45.500 --> 00:38:48.140
And the most important
thing is that some

00:38:48.140 --> 00:38:52.100
of the infra-related
system metrics

00:38:52.100 --> 00:38:58.400
is automatically created
by the Stackdriver agent,

00:38:58.400 --> 00:39:01.160
and it is sending to the
Stackdriver services.

00:39:01.160 --> 00:39:07.130
But we also need another
custom metrics too,

00:39:07.130 --> 00:39:09.920
which is generated from
the application side.

00:39:09.920 --> 00:39:15.440
So one of the things
about the Stackdriver

00:39:15.440 --> 00:39:18.140
is that they can support
log-based metrics.

00:39:18.140 --> 00:39:22.760
And even if we can communicate
with the other application

00:39:22.760 --> 00:39:27.340
developers and we can define
that any metrics in that

00:39:27.340 --> 00:39:31.740
log messages, that it can
gathered by the filtered--

00:39:31.740 --> 00:39:32.900
the log-based metric.

00:39:32.900 --> 00:39:36.530
And it is that one of the
materials for the operation

00:39:36.530 --> 00:39:38.540
and monitoring.

00:39:38.540 --> 00:39:42.290
So now we have the
simple diagram.

00:39:42.290 --> 00:39:45.950
So all logs, which is
stored in the each modules,

00:39:45.950 --> 00:39:52.790
is gathering by the servers and
from the Stackdriver Logging

00:39:52.790 --> 00:39:55.310
agent do that all
the data is gathering

00:39:55.310 --> 00:39:59.240
and transfer that data to
the Stackdriver service.

00:39:59.240 --> 00:40:02.600
And then we can integrate
with the Datadog, one

00:40:02.600 --> 00:40:05.600
of our major
monitoring solutions,

00:40:05.600 --> 00:40:09.770
and that is gathering all
the log-based metrics.

00:40:09.770 --> 00:40:16.000
And it can show the one unified
format of the dashboard.

00:40:16.000 --> 00:40:19.700
It is the-- currently, we are
not going to the SLA practice

00:40:19.700 --> 00:40:20.360
yet.

00:40:20.360 --> 00:40:25.310
So we have the traditional
operation practices

00:40:25.310 --> 00:40:28.730
now so that every tier one
and tier two and even the tier

00:40:28.730 --> 00:40:33.620
three is using that
to get any issue

00:40:33.620 --> 00:40:36.170
detection by our own dashboard.

00:40:36.170 --> 00:40:39.320
Also, we also use the
Stackdriver monitoring tool too

00:40:39.320 --> 00:40:44.780
because that dashboard is also
providing any custom dashboard

00:40:44.780 --> 00:40:45.510
creation.

00:40:45.510 --> 00:40:50.120
And that also can be integrated
with the other solutions,

00:40:50.120 --> 00:40:52.490
like the open source Grafana.

00:40:52.490 --> 00:40:55.760
And we have used
the Slack channel

00:40:55.760 --> 00:41:02.930
to notify every warning or the
complication for convenience.

00:41:02.930 --> 00:41:09.560
And if we can define
that any alert

00:41:09.560 --> 00:41:13.160
in the Stackdriver
and the data row,

00:41:13.160 --> 00:41:17.900
then it can automatically
send that Slack channel,

00:41:17.900 --> 00:41:20.630
and then it can goes
to the stakeholders

00:41:20.630 --> 00:41:27.200
to investigate any issue or for
notify any operation process.

00:41:27.200 --> 00:41:30.530
Basically, the debugging
also can be available

00:41:30.530 --> 00:41:32.600
when we access the log viewer.

00:41:32.600 --> 00:41:37.130
And for analytics purpose,
we are gathering all the log

00:41:37.130 --> 00:41:42.920
messages and filter them
into the Google Cloud Storage

00:41:42.920 --> 00:41:43.610
services.

00:41:43.610 --> 00:41:48.230
And it is going to the
analytics system by Pub/Sub.

00:41:48.230 --> 00:41:54.310
So finally, we can say
that, after applying

00:41:54.310 --> 00:41:56.640
the Stackdriver
for logging system,

00:41:56.640 --> 00:42:01.380
we can have the four
number of effectiveness

00:42:01.380 --> 00:42:03.660
after using that Stackdriver.

00:42:03.660 --> 00:42:07.080
Firstly, we can talk
about the cost saving.

00:42:07.080 --> 00:42:12.000
We can say that at least three
times lower than a cost saving

00:42:12.000 --> 00:42:15.420
was possible when we
used the Stackdriver.

00:42:15.420 --> 00:42:17.790
So it means that we
don't need to think

00:42:17.790 --> 00:42:23.550
about any infra-changes or
maintaining the system, only

00:42:23.550 --> 00:42:28.710
that we can give all effort
to service reliability

00:42:28.710 --> 00:42:30.040
and scalability.

00:42:30.040 --> 00:42:35.700
And it means that secondly, we
can minimize all the workload.

00:42:35.700 --> 00:42:38.880
So we don't need to think
about any maintenance

00:42:38.880 --> 00:42:40.860
about the logging system.

00:42:40.860 --> 00:42:46.300
We can fully give the energy
to service reliability.

00:42:46.300 --> 00:42:50.920
Three, when we use
the Stackdriver,

00:42:50.920 --> 00:42:55.000
we also realize that
unified this logging system

00:42:55.000 --> 00:42:58.960
can be applied to
another project

00:42:58.960 --> 00:43:04.450
if that requirement is really
similar with the Bixby project,

00:43:04.450 --> 00:43:10.060
so that our operation team
has much number of services,

00:43:10.060 --> 00:43:11.290
as Jin mentioned.

00:43:11.290 --> 00:43:13.360
So that, if it is
possible, we can

00:43:13.360 --> 00:43:18.100
add Stackdriver to
the other services

00:43:18.100 --> 00:43:22.700
as the unified platform
of the logging system.

00:43:22.700 --> 00:43:26.560
Finally, we also-- even though
we integrated with the analysis

00:43:26.560 --> 00:43:29.170
data system
internally in Samsung,

00:43:29.170 --> 00:43:31.330
but better scalability.

00:43:31.330 --> 00:43:37.670
And another purpose
of the data analysis

00:43:37.670 --> 00:43:41.520
also is possible when
we're using the BigQuery.

00:43:41.520 --> 00:43:44.600
So this is my last page.

00:43:44.600 --> 00:43:45.720
So I will back to Jin.

00:43:45.720 --> 00:43:46.964
Thank you.

00:43:46.964 --> 00:43:54.840
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:54.840 --> 00:43:56.640
HYUNG JIN LIM: Right.

00:43:56.640 --> 00:43:58.730
I know this last
session, and I know

00:43:58.730 --> 00:44:02.700
you are eager to go back to
your, you know, home and so on.

00:44:02.700 --> 00:44:05.200
But I'll make it
quick as possible.

00:44:05.200 --> 00:44:07.950
But it is my talk
that I wanted to do--

00:44:07.950 --> 00:44:13.020
practicing SRE, trying to make
that happen in our environment

00:44:13.020 --> 00:44:14.530
as well.

00:44:14.530 --> 00:44:16.960
So let's see.

00:44:16.960 --> 00:44:20.370
So some of the challenges as
in Samsung Electronics we had--

00:44:20.370 --> 00:44:23.700
I joined the company in 2013.

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:27.420
I came from the large
software company

00:44:27.420 --> 00:44:28.890
based in Redmond, Washington.

00:44:28.890 --> 00:44:34.230
I spent about 10
to 12 years there.

00:44:34.230 --> 00:44:39.050
Got hired by Samsung and
moved my family to Korea.

00:44:39.050 --> 00:44:41.730
And back then, there were
some other guys like me

00:44:41.730 --> 00:44:46.990
who came from global
software company.

00:44:46.990 --> 00:44:50.880
And then Samsung wanted to
make them an environment where

00:44:50.880 --> 00:44:54.660
software development
management is

00:44:54.660 --> 00:44:59.760
kind of normal to our company
because the company has not

00:44:59.760 --> 00:45:03.030
came from a software base
in their core business.

00:45:03.030 --> 00:45:05.670
Their core business
is manufacturing.

00:45:05.670 --> 00:45:07.630
So they are trying to
create an environment,

00:45:07.630 --> 00:45:13.380
trying to create a culture
of software development.

00:45:13.380 --> 00:45:16.140
But then, as the company
put more investment

00:45:16.140 --> 00:45:20.640
on software development, we
created more service as go on.

00:45:20.640 --> 00:45:23.100
Every time we add more
services, obviously, there

00:45:23.100 --> 00:45:26.830
will be more people to operate
those services as well.

00:45:26.830 --> 00:45:31.620
So as, you know, services, being
more added to the production

00:45:31.620 --> 00:45:35.190
environment, we need more
people to operate and support

00:45:35.190 --> 00:45:37.150
those services.

00:45:37.150 --> 00:45:39.750
The problem is, we
feel that we're not

00:45:39.750 --> 00:45:42.390
going to be able to
justify ourselves

00:45:42.390 --> 00:45:46.140
of growing rate of
operation team any longer.

00:45:46.140 --> 00:45:49.860
So we need to think about new
way of operation still able

00:45:49.860 --> 00:45:55.530
to support a number of services
coming into production.

00:45:55.530 --> 00:45:59.220
As I'm being with
Samsung, I'm kind of

00:45:59.220 --> 00:46:01.440
lucky enough to witness
all the changes being

00:46:01.440 --> 00:46:04.980
done in the software management
inside of Samsung as well.

00:46:04.980 --> 00:46:08.250
As you can see, you
know, box product, right?

00:46:08.250 --> 00:46:16.610
So we ship-- we release flagship
devices every six months,

00:46:16.610 --> 00:46:17.110
right?

00:46:17.110 --> 00:46:18.160
We have S series.

00:46:18.160 --> 00:46:19.600
We have Note series.

00:46:19.600 --> 00:46:21.430
It takes about six months.

00:46:21.430 --> 00:46:24.250
Waterfall methodology of
development lifecycle kind

00:46:24.250 --> 00:46:28.600
of makes sense in box product
in a manufactured system.

00:46:28.600 --> 00:46:30.820
And we have all the
important decisions

00:46:30.820 --> 00:46:35.140
is being made by executives
since we have enough time

00:46:35.140 --> 00:46:37.510
of lifecycle of these products.

00:46:37.510 --> 00:46:41.490
And obviously back
then, we utilized

00:46:41.490 --> 00:46:45.250
a lot of traditional data
centers to host our servers.

00:46:45.250 --> 00:46:49.810
And all the system is sort of
like a monolithic architecture

00:46:49.810 --> 00:46:51.670
system.

00:46:51.670 --> 00:46:53.710
But then over the
year, we know we

00:46:53.710 --> 00:46:59.290
can't do this type of management
any more with the service

00:46:59.290 --> 00:47:00.280
development.

00:47:00.280 --> 00:47:04.330
So we change ourselves to Agile
coming from the Waterfall,

00:47:04.330 --> 00:47:06.730
where we break
down all the tasks.

00:47:06.730 --> 00:47:10.340
We create a backlog and we're
trying fulfill back-- you

00:47:10.340 --> 00:47:13.330
know, given the right
engineer to actually be

00:47:13.330 --> 00:47:15.580
responsible for those backlogs.

00:47:15.580 --> 00:47:19.980
And we run every spring and
trying to ship out at the end

00:47:19.980 --> 00:47:22.620
a shippable product
to production.

00:47:22.620 --> 00:47:26.440
And all the decisions is
now made being made by a PO.

00:47:26.440 --> 00:47:31.430
Or every key feature
in production,

00:47:31.430 --> 00:47:34.270
whether it needs to
be going live or not,

00:47:34.270 --> 00:47:37.240
being decided by
our product owner.

00:47:37.240 --> 00:47:41.635
And obviously, right now,
majority of our servers

00:47:41.635 --> 00:47:46.270
are being hosted in a cloud
unless there are compliance

00:47:46.270 --> 00:47:49.090
that the server has to be in
a traditional data center.

00:47:49.090 --> 00:47:53.350
And you know, why going with
this pattern of the making

00:47:53.350 --> 00:47:55.810
breakdown each feature
in the production?

00:47:55.810 --> 00:47:59.440
It makes sense to architect
ourself as a micro-services

00:47:59.440 --> 00:48:03.710
instead of the monolithic.

00:48:03.710 --> 00:48:08.690
Still, in an old way of
the development lifecycle,

00:48:08.690 --> 00:48:12.850
as I mentioned, we have six
months of time on Waterfall.

00:48:12.850 --> 00:48:14.690
And we could have all the time.

00:48:14.690 --> 00:48:17.960
Every time we have new
service coming in, we can--

00:48:17.960 --> 00:48:23.600
as an operation, we can find
ourselves to ask engineer team,

00:48:23.600 --> 00:48:26.738
create an operation
guide, KB article, and do

00:48:26.738 --> 00:48:29.030
the right training team what's
coming into a production

00:48:29.030 --> 00:48:29.860
environment.

00:48:29.860 --> 00:48:31.530
And it all makes sense.

00:48:31.530 --> 00:48:36.740
But then with the project
managed by Agile and Sprint,

00:48:36.740 --> 00:48:38.310
we don't have that
luxury anymore.

00:48:38.310 --> 00:48:39.890
We don't have time to ask them.

00:48:39.890 --> 00:48:42.620
Or engineering team
does not have a time

00:48:42.620 --> 00:48:44.510
to provide all
those information.

00:48:44.510 --> 00:48:47.780
So we also have a
conflict between business

00:48:47.780 --> 00:48:50.870
and the engineer and
operation, where our priority

00:48:50.870 --> 00:48:54.640
is providing a stable
service to our customers,

00:48:54.640 --> 00:48:57.380
where engineering team is
trying to push new value

00:48:57.380 --> 00:48:59.490
to our production environment.

00:48:59.490 --> 00:49:02.750
So we're continuously
having this conflict.

00:49:02.750 --> 00:49:05.150
While we're trying to
solve this operation

00:49:05.150 --> 00:49:07.820
conflict and the
organizational challenge,

00:49:07.820 --> 00:49:11.240
we came close to chance
to work with the Google

00:49:11.240 --> 00:49:15.320
engineer, Google SRE engineers,
and trying to learn more

00:49:15.320 --> 00:49:20.750
about SRE practice and how
Google does their SRE practice

00:49:20.750 --> 00:49:22.070
in their end.

00:49:22.070 --> 00:49:25.790
And we ran through a lot
of meetings and seminars

00:49:25.790 --> 00:49:27.800
with the Google engineers.

00:49:27.800 --> 00:49:31.900
But then we realized that
Samsung is not Google.

00:49:31.900 --> 00:49:36.240
So what I meant is that we
didn't come from a software

00:49:36.240 --> 00:49:37.330
development company.

00:49:37.330 --> 00:49:43.830
We came from the very
process-driven manufacturer

00:49:43.830 --> 00:49:46.230
culture in the back of our mind.

00:49:46.230 --> 00:49:49.680
So next thing that
we did was, OK,

00:49:49.680 --> 00:49:53.580
so we understand what SRE can
give us, what kind of benefit.

00:49:53.580 --> 00:49:56.430
But then we need to
answer how question.

00:49:56.430 --> 00:50:00.310
How can we implement this
practice to our environment?

00:50:00.310 --> 00:50:03.990
So we can start learning
from other industry leaders

00:50:03.990 --> 00:50:05.520
in practicing SRE.

00:50:05.520 --> 00:50:09.240
We learned from
Netflix, LinkedIn, Uber,

00:50:09.240 --> 00:50:11.440
and many others.

00:50:11.440 --> 00:50:14.430
And they all have something
in common too, right?

00:50:14.430 --> 00:50:18.690
So they break down
to each kind of--

00:50:18.690 --> 00:50:21.330
separate what's
put into production

00:50:21.330 --> 00:50:25.110
and separate who should be
responsible for addressing

00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:26.230
those issues.

00:50:26.230 --> 00:50:29.740
They have a notion of
you build it, you run it.

00:50:29.740 --> 00:50:32.010
So if there is a
developer who is trying

00:50:32.010 --> 00:50:34.275
to push into production,
and that person

00:50:34.275 --> 00:50:38.460
is supposed to know how to
troubleshoot those issues, not

00:50:38.460 --> 00:50:40.060
operation engineers.

00:50:40.060 --> 00:50:45.540
So we really like those notion
of the movement there as well.

00:50:45.540 --> 00:50:48.900
So we came up with our
own version of SRE.

00:50:48.900 --> 00:50:52.320
We call it ourself as a
global service reliability

00:50:52.320 --> 00:50:52.870
engineering.

00:50:52.870 --> 00:50:56.760
And we break down to a different
area of implementation.

00:50:56.760 --> 00:51:00.900
So before, pretty
much every service

00:51:00.900 --> 00:51:03.220
that we are running
today does have SLA.

00:51:03.220 --> 00:51:06.490
We do have a key
index of availability.

00:51:06.490 --> 00:51:09.350
But then that's not
enough for SRE practice.

00:51:09.350 --> 00:51:12.250
We want to make sure
we kind of break down

00:51:12.250 --> 00:51:14.740
to each function
within the service

00:51:14.740 --> 00:51:18.970
and making sure each function,
user impacting function,

00:51:18.970 --> 00:51:22.450
does have an SLO, SLI.

00:51:22.450 --> 00:51:24.520
And also, we want to
make sure that the error

00:51:24.520 --> 00:51:27.280
budget is being communicated
with the engineering team.

00:51:27.280 --> 00:51:31.660
So if you exhaust
all your SLO, then we

00:51:31.660 --> 00:51:34.720
will focus on making
the reliable system

00:51:34.720 --> 00:51:40.080
instead of pushing new
idea into production.

00:51:40.080 --> 00:51:43.790
This is one example of
an SLO document we have.

00:51:43.790 --> 00:51:47.130
As I mentioned, you know,
big service such as Bixby

00:51:47.130 --> 00:51:50.990
has a different functionality
capability within the service.

00:51:50.990 --> 00:51:55.490
One of its capability is ASR,
which translates the utterance

00:51:55.490 --> 00:51:56.570
to text.

00:51:56.570 --> 00:51:59.930
So we create this
template where we break it

00:51:59.930 --> 00:52:04.400
down to an application level
as an ownership of that SLO

00:52:04.400 --> 00:52:07.460
is primary ownership
of SLO is that.

00:52:07.460 --> 00:52:10.160
We have underneath
a primary ownership

00:52:10.160 --> 00:52:13.490
of operation, which is us,
managing infrastructure

00:52:13.490 --> 00:52:15.030
level of SLO.

00:52:15.030 --> 00:52:17.450
And one of the key
points is infrastructure

00:52:17.450 --> 00:52:21.260
SLO is much tighter, higher
than application SLO.

00:52:21.260 --> 00:52:23.420
Obviously, you know,
application SLO

00:52:23.420 --> 00:52:27.800
could not be made if the
underlying framework is not

00:52:27.800 --> 00:52:28.760
there yet, right?

00:52:28.760 --> 00:52:31.640
So that's why we're trying
to kind of differentiate

00:52:31.640 --> 00:52:37.130
between operation primary
responsibility and engineering

00:52:37.130 --> 00:52:40.340
primary responsibility.

00:52:40.340 --> 00:52:42.710
With the Stackdriver
and Datadog,

00:52:42.710 --> 00:52:47.090
we were able to create the
dashboard just for that SLA.

00:52:47.090 --> 00:52:52.280
So based on this SLA, if there
is a SLA, SLO threatening

00:52:52.280 --> 00:52:54.770
event that is happening,
we can integrate

00:52:54.770 --> 00:52:58.520
with the Datadog and Slack and
some other alerting mechanisms.

00:52:58.520 --> 00:53:03.280
We can give the alert
to the right engineers.

00:53:03.280 --> 00:53:06.960
And also, that comes into
instance management as well

00:53:06.960 --> 00:53:11.000
And before we take traditional
multi-tier escalation path,

00:53:11.000 --> 00:53:11.500
right?

00:53:11.500 --> 00:53:14.210
We have tier one,
tier two, tier three,

00:53:14.210 --> 00:53:16.610
which is essentially developers.

00:53:16.610 --> 00:53:19.240
If issue happens,
tier one catches it

00:53:19.240 --> 00:53:21.040
and escalates to
tier two if they

00:53:21.040 --> 00:53:23.830
don't have enough information
how to troubleshoot.

00:53:23.830 --> 00:53:26.760
And tier two in operation
side is supposed to know,

00:53:26.760 --> 00:53:31.150
you know, most, you know,
how feature is running

00:53:31.150 --> 00:53:33.740
in a production environment.

00:53:33.740 --> 00:53:37.640
But if, in the name of the
Agile and the service, you know,

00:53:37.640 --> 00:53:40.480
as I mentioned, there's
always lack of information

00:53:40.480 --> 00:53:43.390
what are running in a production
and application level.

00:53:43.390 --> 00:53:48.380
So we ended up calling developer
pretty much every time.

00:53:48.380 --> 00:53:52.930
So if you think about SLO
of 395 for availability,

00:53:52.930 --> 00:53:56.630
you have only 22 minutes
of allowed downtime.

00:53:56.630 --> 00:53:58.180
So in the middle
of the night, one

00:53:58.180 --> 00:54:00.160
of instances
happening like this,

00:54:00.160 --> 00:54:04.970
it's easy to lose
about 30 minutes.

00:54:04.970 --> 00:54:08.000
So for that month, if we
have a single instance,

00:54:08.000 --> 00:54:09.940
you are violating SLO.

00:54:09.940 --> 00:54:15.040
So we needed to take a different
approach in SRE, right?

00:54:15.040 --> 00:54:19.600
So we're trying to
pinpoint the actual error

00:54:19.600 --> 00:54:21.730
and do a better
job in our logging.

00:54:21.730 --> 00:54:25.180
We may not have to go through
the tier one and tier two.

00:54:25.180 --> 00:54:27.430
If system generates
the right information

00:54:27.430 --> 00:54:31.660
and push those alerts
to the right engineer,

00:54:31.660 --> 00:54:35.770
it could be a
developer because event

00:54:35.770 --> 00:54:38.920
is trying to violate
the SLO for application.

00:54:38.920 --> 00:54:40.960
You could go to our
infrastructure engineers

00:54:40.960 --> 00:54:46.180
if it's violating some SLO
that we pre-built for network,

00:54:46.180 --> 00:54:47.690
right, for example.

00:54:47.690 --> 00:54:50.620
So we can pinpoint the alert.

00:54:50.620 --> 00:54:54.890
And we can finish up doing--

00:54:54.890 --> 00:54:59.590
we bypassed multi-tier
escalation there.

00:54:59.590 --> 00:55:02.980
This I need to finish
up really quick.

00:55:02.980 --> 00:55:04.400
Deploy management.

00:55:04.400 --> 00:55:07.240
You know, same idea.

00:55:07.240 --> 00:55:09.610
Operation team used
to own deployment

00:55:09.610 --> 00:55:10.850
of production environment.

00:55:10.850 --> 00:55:13.650
We call ourselves as a
production engineer, right?

00:55:13.650 --> 00:55:18.030
So we have ownership of making
stable and [INAUDIBLE] there

00:55:18.030 --> 00:55:21.040
for business looking
and customers looking.

00:55:21.040 --> 00:55:24.370
But then as we're
separating out with the SLO

00:55:24.370 --> 00:55:27.520
for developer and
operation engineer,

00:55:27.520 --> 00:55:29.890
now we are making
the infrastructure

00:55:29.890 --> 00:55:32.200
as a code,
configuration as a code.

00:55:32.200 --> 00:55:36.010
Operation have ownership
of pushing new changes

00:55:36.010 --> 00:55:42.090
if it's a configuration
and infrastructure.

00:55:42.090 --> 00:55:44.770
Development team have
ownership of pushing new code

00:55:44.770 --> 00:55:45.940
into production.

00:55:45.940 --> 00:55:49.150
And that way, you know, we
don't have to stop development

00:55:49.150 --> 00:55:51.430
to deploy new code
into production asking

00:55:51.430 --> 00:55:54.460
for a bunch of KB article
to how to troubleshooting

00:55:54.460 --> 00:55:57.810
because this alerting,
when they push new idea

00:55:57.810 --> 00:56:00.190
and if there is a
problem, the alerting

00:56:00.190 --> 00:56:02.380
will go straight
to a developer, not

00:56:02.380 --> 00:56:06.670
to operation who knows
nothing about those changes

00:56:06.670 --> 00:56:09.310
in the code.

00:56:09.310 --> 00:56:12.340
Cultural change is lasting,
but it's not listening.

00:56:12.340 --> 00:56:14.260
This is the hardest
thing to do, right?

00:56:14.260 --> 00:56:18.490
So we can communicate
this to our leadership,

00:56:18.490 --> 00:56:20.680
and they all get the idea.

00:56:20.680 --> 00:56:23.920
And you know first
comment was, you know,

00:56:23.920 --> 00:56:25.780
cultural innovation,
you need to talk

00:56:25.780 --> 00:56:28.750
to make sure all peoples
are in the consensus, right?

00:56:28.750 --> 00:56:32.020
So we are starting to do a
lot of [INAUDIBLE] seminars

00:56:32.020 --> 00:56:35.830
with the front engineering
team pushing this idea

00:56:35.830 --> 00:56:37.510
and make sure they're onboarded.

00:56:37.510 --> 00:56:41.140
And we asked what
needs to be happening

00:56:41.140 --> 00:56:46.380
and what help we're going to
be needing from them as well.

00:56:46.380 --> 00:56:48.550
And some other
things that we did

00:56:48.550 --> 00:56:50.260
was we create a
task force, right?

00:56:50.260 --> 00:56:53.380
So we create a task
force or dedicate a team

00:56:53.380 --> 00:56:56.260
to create the toolset, right?

00:56:56.260 --> 00:56:58.810
We don't do
application deployment,

00:56:58.810 --> 00:57:02.200
we provide the pipelines,
CI/CD pipeline,

00:57:02.200 --> 00:57:05.920
that gradual change is
embedded into a pipeline.

00:57:05.920 --> 00:57:09.390
Risk management is
embedded in the pipeline.

00:57:09.390 --> 00:57:11.860
It's a no brainer for
our development team

00:57:11.860 --> 00:57:14.420
that if they're trying
to push a new idea

00:57:14.420 --> 00:57:17.320
but if it's starting to having
problem, it's easier for them

00:57:17.320 --> 00:57:18.370
to go back as well.

00:57:18.370 --> 00:57:22.300
So we're trying to create a
team providing this type of tool

00:57:22.300 --> 00:57:24.200
to engineering team as well.

00:57:24.200 --> 00:57:28.120
So that same goes with your
logging and monitoring.

00:57:28.120 --> 00:57:32.210
And QA automation-- we don't
have QA automation, security

00:57:32.210 --> 00:57:33.250
automation, yet.

00:57:33.250 --> 00:57:38.320
We have this multi-plan
organizational change

00:57:38.320 --> 00:57:42.250
and trying to fit into
this model of SRE.

00:57:42.250 --> 00:57:44.700
And while we're
trying to provide all

00:57:44.700 --> 00:57:47.750
across the same
toolset for our SRE,

00:57:47.750 --> 00:57:50.980
we also have a service SRE
who are actually working

00:57:50.980 --> 00:57:55.540
on the project, trying to plug
in those platform solutions

00:57:55.540 --> 00:57:57.200
into actual services as well.

00:58:00.780 --> 00:58:03.210
I can't really say
all the benefits

00:58:03.210 --> 00:58:05.430
we are having right now
because we are still

00:58:05.430 --> 00:58:08.220
on the stage on
the adopting SRE.

00:58:08.220 --> 00:58:11.310
But then we are
kind of expecting

00:58:11.310 --> 00:58:15.690
to have a better visibility of
our service with the measuring

00:58:15.690 --> 00:58:18.330
everything that
happening in a production

00:58:18.330 --> 00:58:22.110
and reducing mean time to
recover, obviously, right?

00:58:22.110 --> 00:58:26.550
So trying to meet the SRO
and reduce the release time

00:58:26.550 --> 00:58:29.340
with the CI/CD
pipeline automation

00:58:29.340 --> 00:58:33.880
and optimize operation
resource as well.

00:58:33.880 --> 00:58:36.240
So I think this is
my last session.

00:58:36.240 --> 00:58:41.040
Thank you for joining us, and
you may go to your family now.

00:58:41.040 --> 00:58:43.490
[MUSIC PLAYING]

